n$$\n(\\frac{3}{1})=[]+(\\frac{2}{1})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $(\\frac{3}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{3}{1})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{3}{1})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{1})=(\\frac{2}{0})+(\\frac{2}{1})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{2}{0}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c976b6a-4523-51e1-a243-27aa8937db25" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Relationship Between the Rows of Pascal's Triangle in Terms of Binomial Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42eec711-2366-5e9e-b394-97fc17f3a880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333612} {:answer/id #uuid "48cd114b-2976-5998-8aab-6731eae06237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333660} {:answer/id #uuid "56a6c0b8-3c50-57e0-a732-7b6b3ad0a73b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333773} {:answer/id #uuid "75f609f8-4a1f-5d10-a85e-5c13f336ab51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{0})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334055} {:answer/id #uuid "aace99ba-6af1-5dd7-8bf4-07b6ec0c5a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{0})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48cd114b-2976-5998-8aab-6731eae06237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333660} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ac1b6d-c8df-5a4f-8cb7-7837698f5128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "911738be-601f-52ff-bdd9-bde88253aa9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-69722" :question/problem "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{2})=(\\frac{2}{1})+[]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $(\\frac{3}{2}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{3}{2})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{3}{2})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{2})=(\\frac{2}{1})+(\\frac{2}{2})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{2}{2}).$"}]}
